  texture_mask: {fileID: 0}
  title: FBI Load
  text: 'In the 1950''s and 60''s, postwar America faced an increased number of violent
    crimes and armed confrontations between gangsters and police officers. Contemporary
    policemen were mainly firing sidearms chambered in .38 Special, with the standard
    load being a 158-grain lead round-nose bullet, which had the.. annoying tendency
    to go through whatever -or whoever- it was fired at, and continue on through
    whatever (or whoever) happened to be standing behind, all the while failing to
    actually stop the intended target.


    Despite making numerous complaints,
    cries of police unions went largely unanswered, with a few agencies attempting
    to introduce new .38 Special loads to combat this problem, such as the 200-grain
    blunt-nosed "Super Police" load, which faced the.. opposite problem. Sure, the
    bullet would cause Massive Internal Damage to whoever it hit, but bullets that
    missed had the annoying tendency to ricochet and hit whatever or whoever happened
    to be standing nearby.


    In 1972, the Federal Bureau of Investigation unveiled
    their new standard issue +P load, later dubbed the "FBI Load". This load contained
    more, (and, more powerful) gunpowder, as well as a 158-grain unjacketed all-lead
    semi-wadcutter hollow-point bullet. These improvements, when fired from a standard
    law enforcement revolver, allowed the .38 Special cartridge to effectively and
    consistently take down whatever (or whoever) it was fired at. This load was very
    quickly adopted by practically all American police departments, and remained
    the primary round in use by the FBI until the mid 1980''s, when a slew of tragic
    and deadly shootouts proved the need to switch to higher capacity semi-automatic
    handguns. '
  end_padding_seconds: 0
