  _help_entry_name: ciarencew.+p_ammo
  _help_entry_info_sprite: {fileID: 21300000, guid: ffbd1986a20de74cc9d6349f36e53d13,
    type: 3}
  _help_entry_title: Overpressure Ammunition
  _help_entry_description: 'Some of our more advanced Receivers have opted to include
    overpressure ammunition (more commonly refered to as +P) in their caches. While
    our firearms training did not include using those types of ammunition -- you
    might be able to make good use of them. Due to their increased gunpowder load,
    the bullets fired from those rounds have higher velocity, and, commonly, higher
    mass. All this to say, whatever you hit, will get hit <u><b>hard</b></u>; A turret
    you graze might still topple over -- A shock drone you hit center mass might
    crash into a wall, disabling it.


    Despite these advantages over more "standard"
    rounds, you need to be aware that +P ammo also come with a lot more recoil than
    you might expect (my gun almost jumped out my hand the first time I shot one
    of those), and their increased pressure might prevent the gun from functioning
    properly, resulting in more frequent malfunctions.'
  _competition_help_entry_name: ciarencew.-p_ammo
  _competition_help_entry_info_sprite: {fileID: 21300000, guid: af2a87d66b080ba808d13be2d24e3549,
    type: 3}
  _competition_help_entry_title: Underpressure Ammunition
  _competition_help_entry_description: 'While standard and overpressure loads might
    be useful for self-defense, we recommend novice Receivers to practice their firearm
    drills using reduced-recoil loads. These loads have a lesser amount of gunpowder,
    and, more often that not, a lighter bullet. As the name suggests, this reduces
    the recoil exerted upon the gun when it fires. Such loads are also useful for
    competition matches, although so called "match grade" ammo usually have a heavier
    bullet. Do note however that while the gun will kick back with less force, so
    will the slide, which might cause the feed and eject mechanisms to fail.


    Obviously,
    this makes these rounds less effective against killdrones, so make sure to make
    your shots count.'
